<section class="hero"><div class="wrap hero-in"><div>
<p class="kick">Cook &middot; Taste &middot; Repeat</p>
<h1>Dinner gets easier when you stop following and start understanding.</h1>
<p class="lead">Yumster is a home-cooking site with three free tools: what goes with what, what to use when the cupboard says no, and how to cook for the number of people who actually turned up.</p>
<p class="hero-cta"><a class="btn" href="#tools">Open the tools</a> <a class="btn gh" href="everyday-cooking-guide.html">Read the cooking guide</a></p></div>
<figure class="hero-fig"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1638866281450-3933540af86a?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=75" alt="A bowl of ramen with meat, eggs, noodles and vegetables" width="800" height="800" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption class="hero-tag">3 free tools</figcaption></figure></div></section>
<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap">
<div class="chips"><span>8 ingredients paired</span><span>24 tested swaps</span><span>Scales 1&ndash;20 servings</span><span>No sign-up</span></div>
<div class="sec-h"><p class="kick">Why bother</p><h2>Three habits beat a hundred recipes</h2><p>Nobody remembers two hundred recipes. People who cook well have a small set of habits they repeat, and everything else is improvisation on top.</p></div>
<div class="three">
<div class="pill"><div class="em">01</div><h3>Taste as you go</h3><p>Season in layers, not at the end. A spoon and a pinch of salt will fix more dishes than any new ingredient.</p></div>
<div class="pill"><div class="em">02</div><h3>Know your swaps</h3><p>A missing ingredient should not end dinner. Most things have two or three stand-ins that behave almost the same.</p></div>
<div class="pill"><div class="em">03</div><h3>Cook for the table</h3><p>Recipes are written for four whether you are two or nine. Scale on purpose and the pan stops lying to you.</p></div>
</div></div></section>
<section class="tools" id="tools"><div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-h"><p class="kick">Free tools</p><h2>Work it out in the kitchen</h2><p>Everything runs in your browser. Nothing is saved and nothing is sent anywhere.</p></div>
<div class="tool" id="pair"><h3>Flavour Pairing Finder</h3><p>Pick an ingredient and see the partners that always work, the ones worth trying, and the technique that matters most.</p>
<div class="tool-grid"><div><div class="fld"><label for="pIng">Ingredient</label><select id="pIng"><option value="tomato">Tomato</option><option value="lemon">Lemon</option><option value="garlic">Garlic</option><option value="ginger">Ginger</option><option value="mushroom">Mushroom</option><option value="chickpea">Chickpea</option><option value="rice">Rice</option><option value="yoghurt">Yoghurt</option></select></div>
<figure style="margin:0"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1597958792579-bd3517df6399?auto=format&fit=crop&w=700&q=75" alt="Vegetable salad in a white ceramic bowl" width="700" height="470" loading="lazy" style="border-radius:16px;aspect-ratio:3/2;object-fit:cover"></figure></div>
<div class="out" id="pOut"></div></div></div>
<div class="tool" id="swap"><h3>Pantry Swap Finder</h3><p>Out of something halfway through? Here are three swaps that work and what each one changes.</p>
<div class="tool-grid"><div><div class="fld"><label for="swIng">What have you run out of?</label><select id="swIng"><option value="egg">Egg</option><option value="butter">Butter</option><option value="milk">Milk or buttermilk</option><option value="cream">Cream</option><option value="stock">Stock</option><option value="breadcrumb">Breadcrumbs</option><option value="sugar">Sugar</option><option value="soy">Soy sauce</option></select></div>
<figure style="margin:0"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1604497198754-a2183225cdc4?auto=format&fit=crop&w=700&q=75" alt="A white ceramic bowl of food" width="700" height="470" loading="lazy" style="border-radius:16px;aspect-ratio:3/2;object-fit:cover"></figure></div>
<div class="out" id="swOut"></div></div></div>
<div class="tool" id="scale"><h3>Portion Scaler</h3><p>Turn a recipe written for four into one written for the people at your table, with the warnings that come with it.</p>
<div class="tool-grid"><div><div class="fgrid"><div class="fld"><label for="scBase">Recipe serves</label><input id="scBase" type="number" min="1" max="40" value="4"></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="scWant">You are feeding</label><input id="scWant" type="number" min="1" max="40" value="6"></div></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="scApp">Appetite</label><select id="scApp"><option value="0.8">Light &mdash; starter or small plates</option><option value="1" selected>Normal &mdash; a usual dinner</option><option value="1.25">Hearty &mdash; hungry people, no leftovers</option><option value="1.5">Generous &mdash; seconds and leftovers</option></select></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="scType">Type of dish</label><select id="scType"><option value="stew">Stew, soup or curry</option><option value="bake">Cake or bake</option><option value="rice">Rice or grains</option><option value="fry">Fried or pan-cooked</option></select></div>
<figure style="margin:0"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1569760142069-bc6838de16c1?auto=format&fit=crop&w=700&q=75" alt="A plate of vegetable salad" width="700" height="470" loading="lazy" style="border-radius:16px;aspect-ratio:3/2;object-fit:cover"></figure></div>
<div class="out" id="scOut"></div></div></div>
</div></section>
<section class="band"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1631709497146-a239ef373cf1?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1600&q=75" alt="" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy">
<div class="wrap"><p class="kick" style="color:#EAF3DC">The short version</p><h2>Salt, acid and heat do most of the work</h2>
<p>If a dish tastes dull, it is almost never missing a rare ingredient. It is under-salted, it needs a squeeze of something sharp, or the pan was not hot enough.</p>
<p style="margin-top:1.3em"><a class="btn" style="background:#fff;color:#2E2A26" href="flavour-pairing-guide.html">Read the pairing guide</a></p></div></section>
<section><div class="wrap"><div class="sec-h"><p class="kick">Long reads</p><h2>Two guides worth your evening</h2></div>
<div class="cards">
<article class="card"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1731460202531-bf8389d565f7?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=75" alt="A bowl filled with noodles, meat and vegetables" width="800" height="500" loading="lazy"><div class="cb"><p class="kick">Guide one</p><h3>Everyday Cooking Guide</h3><p>Seasoning, heat, timing and the handful of techniques that make weeknight food taste like somebody meant it. Plus how to build a meal from what is already in the house.</p><p><a class="btn gh" href="everyday-cooking-guide.html">Read the guide</a></p></div></article>
<article class="card"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1683062332605-4e1209d75346?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=75" alt="Three tacos on a plate beside a glass" width="800" height="500" loading="lazy"><div class="cb"><p class="kick">Guide two</p><h3>Flavour Pairing Guide</h3><p>Why certain combinations work, how to balance fat, acid, salt and sweetness, and how to invent a dish that tastes deliberate rather than random.</p><p><a class="btn gh" href="flavour-pairing-guide.html">Read the guide</a></p></div></article>
</div></div></section>
<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap"><div class="sec-h"><p class="kick">Questions</p><h2>Things people ask us</h2></div>
<div class="faq"><details><summary>How do I know which flavours go together?</summary><p>Most classic pairings share aroma compounds or balance each other: fat against acid, sweet against salt, soft against crunchy. Start from a pairing people have cooked for a hundred years, then change one thing. Our pairing finder lists the safe partners and the more interesting ones separately.</p></details><details><summary>Can I swap an ingredient in any recipe?</summary><p>Usually, but the swap changes texture as well as flavour. Swapping butter for oil in a cake keeps it moist and loses the crumb; swapping cream for yoghurt adds tang and risks splitting. Change one ingredient at a time so you know what caused the difference.</p></details><details><summary>Does doubling a recipe mean doubling everything?</summary><p>Not quite. Flour, liquid and most vegetables scale in a straight line. Salt, chilli and strong spices do not, so add about three quarters and correct at the end. Cooking times change far less than quantities do.</p></details><details><summary>Why does my food taste flat even when I follow the recipe?</summary><p>Almost always salt or acid. Season in layers as you cook rather than all at the end, and finish with a squeeze of lemon or a splash of vinegar. A flat dish is rarely missing an exotic ingredient.</p></details><details><summary>Do I need special equipment to cook well?</summary><p>No. A heavy pan that holds heat, a sharp knife, a board and a set of scales cover almost everything on this site. Scales matter more than any gadget, because volume measurements for flour vary by up to a third.</p></details></div></div></section>
